1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 {... etc. "There were some vicissitudes in the game
between Crakanthorp and Griffith. The latter quite early won a piece by a
clever manoeuvre at a cost of the exchange, and should have easily scored.
Later on, however, he lost a piece by a sheer oversight. Fortunately for him,
he had a majority of pawns, and at the adjournment he was endeavouring to win
a difficult endgame, in which he had rook and four pawns against rook and
three... evening session... Griffith tried hard to win his rook and pawn
ending with Crakanthorp, and the game should have been drawn, but Crakanthorp
missed his way, and Griffith gained an important point." (Sheffield Daily
Telegraph, 15 August 1912)} 0-1

