1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 e6 3. e3 c5 4. Bd3 Nc6 5. b3 Nf6 6. Bb2 cxd4 7. exd4 Bd6 8. Ne5
O-O 9. O-O Nb4 10. f4 Nxd3 11. cxd3 Nd7 12. Rf3 f6 13. Ng4 Re8 14. Qe1 Qe7 15.
Nc3 Qf7 16. Nb5 Bb8 17. Ba3 Nf8 18. Qg3 Kh8 19. Nd6 Bxd6 20. Bxd6 Bd7 21. Raf1
Qg6 22. Qh4 h6 23. Rg3 Qf7 {"Griffith had a piece of good fortune in his game
with Wahltuch. He got the worst of the opening and then was badly hemmed in in
the middle game, while Wahltuch had an attack which seemed to require merely
care and patience to push to a successful conclusion. Wahltuch, however,
ventured on a sacrifice which, although quite powerful in appearance, was not
quite sound, and Griffith was able to escape by giving up the queen for two
more pieces." (Times, 22 Aug 1912)} 24. Ne5 $5 fxe5 25. Bxe5 Kh7 26. Rxg7+ Qxg7
27. Bxg7 Kxg7 28. Rf3 Ng6 29. Rg3 Rf8 30. Qg4 Rf6 31. h4 Rg8 32. h5 Kh7 33.
hxg6+ $2 Rfxg6 {"and Black has a winning advantage. At the evening sitting,
however, the game had an extraordinary ending. Griffith should have won with
ease, but being aware that no harm could come to him with ordinary care, he
took matters rather leisurely and finally when he had Wahltuch's king in a
mating net, he allowed his opponent to secure a draw by stalemate by a
sacrifice of the queen." (The Times, ibid.)} 34. Qh4 Rxg3 35. f5 Rxg2+ 36. Kf1
R2g5 37. fxe6 Bxe6 38. Ke1 Re8 39. Qf4 Bd7+ 40. Kd2 Rg2+ 41. Kc3 Rc8+ 42. Kb4
Be6 43. a4 Rg6 44. Qe5 a6 45. Ka5 Rcg8 46. b4 R8g7 47. b5 axb5 48. axb5 Bg8 49.
b6 Rd7 50. Kb4 Rdd6 51. Qe7+ Kh8 52. Qe5+ Rgf6 53. Ka4 Rxb6 54. Ka5 Rbe6 55.
Qg3 Rc6 56. Kb4 Rc1 57. Kb3 Rb6+ 58. Ka2 Rc2+ 59. Ka1 Re2 $4 60. Qg7+ Kxg7 {
Sources: (moves 1-34) The Scotsman, 23 August 1912, p7 & The Times, 23 August
1912 & Liverpool Daily Post, 23 August 1912; Year-Book of Chess (Ed. EA
Michell) 1913, p157 (but gives colours wrong in main title); (moves 22-32 but
seemingly misnumbered) Evening Mail, 23 August 1912} 1/2-1/2

